2 The 40-Year Purist Guarantee
******************************

Every stock GNU Emacs keychord works exactly as documented in the
official GNU Emacs manual:
   • ‘C-x C-f’ finds files, ‘C-x b’ switches buffers, ‘C-s’ searches.
   • ‘M-x’ executes commands, ‘C-g’ cancels, ‘C-h’ provides help.
   • ‘TAB’ indents, ‘C-M-i’ invokes completion-at-point.
   • ‘C-x 2’, ‘C-x 3’, ‘C-x 0’, ‘C-x 1’, ‘C-x o’ manage windows.
   • ‘C-x t’ manages tabs and workspaces.

   Custom Hellmacs commands are grouped strictly under ‘C-c’, the prefix
reserved by GNU Emacs conventions for users:
‘C-c h’
     Hellmacs core commands (‘C-c h s’ Altar, ‘C-c h f’ Forge, ‘C-c h k’
     IntelliJ Finder, ‘C-c h i’ Info manual, ‘C-c h p’ Relic Chamber).
‘C-c c’
     Code and compilation commands (‘C-c c c’ compile, ‘C-c c a’ code
     actions, ‘C-c c r’ rename).
‘C-c l’
     The Localleader: mode-specific commands (Java imports,
     refactorings, test runners).
‘C-c w’
     Workspace and tab management (‘C-c w p’ open project tab, ‘C-c w w’
     switch tab).
‘C-c t’
     Toggles (‘C-c t w’ tab-bar, ‘C-c t l’ line numbers, ‘C-c t f’
     flymake).


